* React19 has support for Document Metadata. Means we can put elements that usually go on the <head> element directly inside our React component.
  - <title>, <link>, <meta>

* Before React19, we had to put elements inside a <Helmet> element.
  - return (
    <>
      <Helmet>
        <title>Ecommerce Project</title>
      </Helmet>
    </>
  )

* ref as a prop as of React19.
  - We can use "ref" as an element and access it like a prop.

  - EX: useRef()

* React Compiler = optimizes our React code.
  - BEFORE: When a component updates, it will re-create all the components inside. Lead to performance issues.
    - We had to use useMemo(), useCallback(), and memo() to prevent a component from being re-created.

  - AFTER: React Compiler adds these optimizations automatically.

* How to Setup the React Compiler
  - Run this cmd in ecommerce-project cmd line:
    - "npm install --save-dev babel-plugin-react-compiler@rc"

  - The copy react-config into "vite.config.js"

  - Check if React Compiler is Working
    
    - 1) Start the backend using npm run dev.
    - 2) Start the frontend using npm run dev.
    - 3) Install the React DevTools Chrome extension.
    - 4) In your project (open the URL provided by Vite in the browser), open the Console, and open the "Components" tab.
    - 5) If there's a badge beside the components called "Memo ✨" the React Compiler is working.

* Removed "propTypes" and "defaultProps" for functions
  - propTypes = lets us check the type of a prop.
    - NOW, we use TypeScript to check prop types.

  - defaultProps = lets us set a default value for an undefined prop. We can now do this w. normal JavaScript.

* Set up TypeScript with React.
  - Use create vite command

  - Make sure to run in react-course folder

  - "npx create-vite@6.5.0"

  - Create the project "ecommerce-project"

  - Select framework "React"

  - Select variant "TypeScript"

  - Inside "src" folder, we now have a "App.tsx" file.

  - Install packages and run dev

* TypeScript = JavaScript with extra features.

* TypeScript lets us add TYPES to our variables.
  - EX: Tell TS "message" is a string variable
    - const message: string = 'hello';

    - We can use string methods now like:
      - message.toLowerCase();

* TypeScript has Type Checking. TS will check if your code works with specific type values and functions.
  - EX: Would ERROR out.
    - message.toFixed();
  
* Type Inference = TypeScript figures out the type automatically.
  - EX: We remove the "string" and TS can infer the type is a string.
    - const message = 'hello';

  - EX: Infers "count" is a number due to the state.
    - const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

    - count.toLowerCase();
    
